# The trust root for over-the-air updates.
#
# This is the PUBLIC half of the release signing key. Its private half must
# live on the maintainer's machine, passphrase-protected, and never touch this
# device, GitHub, or CI. A release is accepted only if it carries a signature
# made by the matching private key, in the "flightradar" namespace.
#
# "Passphrase-protected" is a requirement, not something this file can check.
# It was briefly untrue: the key was first generated without a passphrase, and
# releases up to 2026.09.13.3 were signed in that state. It is protected now.
# Every unit that trusts this key trusts whoever can read that file, so if it
# is ever regenerated or copied, re-protect it with
#   ssh-keygen -p -f ~/.ssh/flightradar-signing
# which re-encrypts the existing key in place -- same key, same public half,
# nothing to re-sign.
#
# This file must be present on the device BEFORE it ships. Fetching the key
# over the same channel as the update would make the signature meaningless --
# whoever could tamper with one could tamper with the other.
#
# GitHub is delivery, not trust. A compromised account or token could publish a
# release; it could not sign one, and the device would refuse it.
#
# To rotate: generate a new key, ship an update signed by the OLD key that
# replaces this file, then start signing with the new one. Rotating in the
# other order strands every device.
flightradar-release ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIGwjYgspX5EgL4ECYiqh2IZz2lnt/NiVS5QS6ngwf8T/ flightradar release signing
